WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:05.691
Now we deliberately challenge the database rules The
examples test

00:00:05.717 --> 00:00:11.506
duplicate natural keys negative prices orphan
foreign-key references and impossible time ranges

00:00:13.363 --> 00:00:18.648
These are useful tests because a good design should
reject them Run

00:00:18.674 --> 00:00:23.967
the provided invalid example and read the PostgreSQL
error Do not treat

00:00:23.993 --> 00:00:29.102
the rejection as a failed lesson Then connect the
error to the

00:00:29.115 --> 00:00:34.612
constraint responsible for it A strong schema is not
only one that

00:00:34.639 --> 00:00:39.997
accepts valid data It is also one that reliably
refuses invalid data

00:00:41.726 --> 00:00:45.633
When designing your own tables plan at least one
invalid-data test for

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:50.320
each important rule That helps confirm the guardrail
actually works
